WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.690
OMER MAHMOOD: Hey, Mark.

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:01.170
How's it going?

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.120
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Hey, Omer.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:04.380
I'm just waiting on these
deep machine learning models

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.515
to train using TensorFlow,
but it's taking forever.

00:00:06.515 --> 00:00:08.810
Do you know any way we could
possibly speed them up?

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:09.682
OMER MAHMOOD: Sure.

00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:11.890
Well, one approach is to
use Tensor Processing Units,

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.495
or TPUs, that enable
you to train and run

00:00:14.495 --> 00:00:15.870
predictions on
TensorFlow machine

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.320
learning models
faster than ever.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:17.730
MARK MIRCHANDANI: OK.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:18.670
Sounds pretty good.

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.390
But what exactly is a TPU?

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:21.360
I've heard of GPUs.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.060
But what does a
TPU do differently?

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.024
OMER MAHMOOD: Well
here, let me show you.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:28.398
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.150
Here we have a comparison
of a GPU and TPU.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:39.620
The modern GPU usually has 2,500
to 5,000 arithmetic logic units

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.420
in a single processor.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.970
That means you could execute
thousands of multiplications

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.820
and additions simultaneously.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.490
But the GPU is still a
general-purpose processor

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.010
that has to support millions
of different applications

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.980
and software.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.190
This means that for
every single calculation,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.820
the GPU needs to access
registers, or shared memory,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.220
to read and store the
intermediate calculation

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.300
results, causing a bottleneck.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.440
TPUs can't run word processors,
control rocket engines,

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.360
or execute bank transactions.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.210
But they can handle massive
multiplications and additions

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.060
for neural networks at
blazingly fast speeds

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.850
while consuming much less power
and inside a smaller physical

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.730
footprint.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.500
So let's look at
actually setting one up.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.100
First, we need to launch a Cloud
Shell in the Cloud console.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.020
We're going to use a TPU tool.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.270
Run the following command
to set up a Compute Engine

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.740
virtual machine and a Cloud
TPU with associated services.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.570
This combination of
resources and services

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.790
is called a Cloud TPU flock.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.990
You can use a CTPU up command
and the output message.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.450
The name is the name of
your TPU resource, which

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.760
defaults to your
username, and the zone

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.360
is a default geographic zone
for your Compute Engine.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.090
Once created, the CTPU
command connects us

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.913
to the compute instance
with Cloud TPU attached.

00:01:57.913 --> 00:01:59.080
MARK MIRCHANDANI: OK, great.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.970
So we have a TPU, but how do
we check that everything's

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:01.603
up and running?

00:02:01.603 --> 00:02:02.770
OMER MAHMOOD: Good question.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.770
We're going to test the
environment by executing

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.650
a simple TensorFlow script.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:08.530
This script is read
in Python and performs

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.509
a simple computation.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:12.102
Let's run that script now.

00:02:12.102 --> 00:02:14.560
Of course, you don't want to
leave these resources running,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.670
so you'd better make sure to
spin them down when we're done.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.680
First, we'll disconnect
from the Compute Engine VM

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:21.988
with the exit command.

00:02:21.988 --> 00:02:23.530
Then we'll run this
command to delete

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.500
the VM and its
associated cloud TPU--

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.570
CTPU delete.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.630
And then add the
name of your VM.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:34.210
TPUs are specifically made
to make matrix calculations

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:34.948
faster.

00:02:34.948 --> 00:02:36.490
So you definitely
want to attach them

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.530
to any VM that is used
for machine learning.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.420
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Don't forget
to subscribe for more great

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.670
Google Cloud Platform content.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

